I'm not sure how on-topic this is here (it's based mostly on D&D3.x),
but it's about how weapons could vary more in their effect than they do
in nethack currently.

In D&D3.x, weapons vary in more than just the damage roll.  They vary by
base damage roll, frequency of critical, and size of critical.  Weight's
also a factor, some (can) use Dex instead of Str for the attack modifier
(IIRC nethack *always* uses Dex, which I'm okay with -- I'm tempted to
do the same for my D&D houserules), some are easier to use in the
offhand (#twoweapon gets easier), and so on.

Material can make more of a difference sometimes.  Adamantine weapons
ignore handness less than 20 (adamantine itself, IOW); this could do
some *happy* things against iron and stone golems.  Adamantine armor
gives damage resistance (DR) against attacks.  Mithral items (metal
only) are just as effective as normal, but at half the weight.

I don't know how hard it would be to implement, but there are also
'bastard' weapons -- usually used two-handed, but with training ('basic
skill' in nethack?) may be used one-handed.

